A production script has a shape: shebang, 'set -euo pipefail', constants at the top, small 'local'-using functions, a main() that sequences them, logging with timestamps, and meaningful exit codes. Here is a disk-and-service health check in that shape: '#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail
LOG=/var/log/healthcheck.log
log() { echo "$(date '+%F %T') $*" | tee -a "$LOG"; }
check_disk() { local use; use=$(df --output=pcent / | tail -1 | tr -dc 0-9); if [ "$use" -gt 85 ]; then log "FAIL disk ${use}%"; return 1; fi; log "OK disk ${use}%"; }
check_ssh() { if systemctl is-active --quiet sshd; then log "OK sshd"; else log "FAIL sshd"; return 1; fi }
main() { local rc=0; check_disk || rc=1; check_ssh || rc=1; exit $rc; }
main "$@"'. Worked example: walk the failure path when disk is at 91 percent. Solution: check_disk computes use=91, the -gt 85 test is true, log writes '2026-10-07 17:30:00 FAIL disk 91%' to both terminal (tee) and the log file, returns 1; main records rc=1 via '||' without tripping 'set -e' (failures in || lists are exempt); check_ssh still runs; main exits 1. The caller -- cron, Jenkins, a human -- sees exit 1 and the log explains exactly which check failed.

Python's for loop iterates over any iterable, while while loops on a condition — but the panel-relevant subtleties are break, continue, and the rarely taught else clause on loops. break exits the loop immediately; continue skips to the next iteration; the else block after a loop runs only if the loop finished without hitting break. Worked example — scanning hosts for the first failure:

hosts = ["web1", "web2", "web3"]
status = {"web1": "up", "web2": "down", "web3": "up"}
for h in hosts:
    if status[h] == "down":
        print("first failure:", h)
        break
else:
    print("all healthy")

Solution: prints "first failure: web2" and the else never runs, because break fired. If every host were "up", the loop would complete and print "all healthy". Worked example two — continue filters without nesting:

for code in [200, 500, 200, 503]:
    if code == 200:
        continue
    print("alert on", code)

Solution: prints "alert on 500" then "alert on 503" — the 200s are skipped before the print. Read continue as "skip the rest of this round" and break as "stop looping entirely," and always check whether a loop has an else before predicting output — panels love hiding the else in plain sight.

